(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("sql-ops",{"sql-ops/reading-explain-and-finding-slow-queries":{"html":"\u003cp>Most database slowness comes from a small number of queries. The work is to find them, read what the database does with them, and change one thing at a time. The examples use PostgreSQL, and the ideas carry over to MySQL and others.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Finding expensive queries with pg_stat_statements and the slow query log\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading EXPLAIN and EXPLAIN ANALYZE\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sequential scan, index scan and join types\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What stale statistics look like\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-queries-first\">Find the queries first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Enable the \u003ccode>pg_stat_statements\u003c/code> extension. It records every normalized query with how many times it ran and how long it took.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time::numeric,0) AS total_ms, round(mean_exec_time::numeric,1) AS mean_ms, left(query,60) AS query FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 3;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time::numeric,0) AS total_ms, round(mean_exec_time::numeric,1) AS mean_ms, left(query,60) AS query FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 3;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> calls  | total_ms | mean_ms | query\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--------+----------+---------+--------------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 480211 |  9120432 |    19.0 | SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = $1 ORDER BY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   1210 |  4411200 |  3645.5 | SELECT customer_id, sum(total) FROM orders WHERE cre\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Sort by \u003cstrong>total\u003c/strong> time, not by the slowest single run. A 19 ms query that runs 480 000 times costs more than a 3.6 s report that runs 1 200 times. The setting \u003ccode>log_min_duration_statement = 500\u003c/code> writes any statement slower than 500 ms to the log, which catches queries your extension view has not reached yet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"explain\">EXPLAIN\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>EXPLAIN\u003c/code> shows the plan the database chooses. \u003ccode>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\u003c/code> runs the query and shows what really happened. Do not use ANALYZE on a statement that changes data without a transaction you roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Seq Scan on orders  (cost=0.00..412345.00 rows=11 width=96) (actual time=0.031..1893.2 rows=9 loops=1)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   Filter: (customer_id = 4711)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   Rows Removed by Filter: 9999991\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   Buffers: shared hit=1204 read=211141\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Planning Time: 0.12 ms\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Execution Time: 1893.3 ms\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read it from the inside out and look for:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Seq Scan\u003c/strong> on a big table that returns a few rows. The database read ten million rows to find nine (\u003ccode>Rows Removed by Filter\u003c/code>). An index on \u003ccode>customer_id\u003c/code> would let it jump to them.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Index Scan / Index Only Scan / Bitmap Heap Scan\u003c/strong>: the database uses an index. Fewer buffers read is the goal.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Estimated rows vs actual rows\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>rows=11\u003c/code> versus \u003ccode>rows=9\u003c/code> is fine. When the estimate is 10 and the actual is 2 000 000, the planner made its choice with wrong information. Run \u003ccode>ANALYZE table;\u003c/code> to refresh statistics.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sort\u003c/strong> with \u003ccode>Sort Method: external merge Disk\u003c/code> means the sort spilled to disk because \u003ccode>work_mem\u003c/code> was too small for that query.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Nested Loop\u003c/strong> is good for few rows and terrible for many. \u003cstrong>Hash Join\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>Merge Join\u003c/strong> suit larger sets.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"change-one-thing\">Change one thing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After a change, run the same EXPLAIN again and compare the time and the buffers. Keep the old plan text in your ticket, so a reviewer can see the difference.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);&quot;\npsql -c &quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;&quot; | grep -E &quot;Index|Execution&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CREATE INDEX\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;&quot;\u003c/span> | grep -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Index|Execution&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Index Scan using idx_orders_customer on orders  (actual time=0.040..0.061 rows=9 loops=1)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Execution Time: 0.09 ms\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code> builds the index without blocking writes. It takes longer and cannot run inside a transaction block.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-query-that-was-fast-yesterday\">Incident: The query that was fast yesterday\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A checkout query went from 5 ms to 4 seconds overnight with no code change.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM carts WHERE status='open' AND user_id=88;&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT relname, last_autoanalyze, n_live_tup FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname='carts';&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM carts WHERE status=&#x27;open&#x27; AND user_id=88;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Nested Loop  (cost=0.85..120.40 rows=3 width=64) (actual time=0.03..4012.7 rows=118002 loops=1)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT relname, last_autoanalyze, n_live_tup FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname=&#x27;carts&#x27;;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> carts | 2026-10-19 02:11:04 | 3120\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The planner expected 3 rows and found 118 002. The table had been loaded with a large import the night before, and statistics were two weeks old, so the planner chose a nested loop that suited a small table. \u003ccode>ANALYZE carts;\u003c/code> fixed the plan in seconds. The lesson is to compare estimated and actual rows before adding indexes: a missing index and stale statistics look similar and have different cures.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tuning the slowest single query and ignoring the one that runs a million times\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding indexes before reading the plan\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running EXPLAIN ANALYZE on writes in production without a rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Judging by a query on a small development database\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"find-the-queries-first","text":"Find the queries first"},{"id":"explain","text":"EXPLAIN"},{"id":"change-one-thing","text":"Change one thing"},{"id":"incident-the-query-that-was-fast-yesterday","text":"Incident: The query that was fast yesterday"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Using EXPLAIN","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: pg_stat_statements","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html"},{"t":"Use The Index, Luke","u":"https://use-the-index-luke.com/"}],"text":"Most database slowness comes from a small number of queries. The work is to find them, read what the database does with them, and change one thing at a time. The examples use PostgreSQL, and the ideas carry over to MySQL and others. What you will learn Finding expensive queries with pg_stat_statements and the slow query log Reading EXPLAIN and EXPLAIN ANALYZE Sequential scan, index scan and join types What stale statistics look like Find the queries first Enable the pg_stat_statements extension. It records every normalized query with how many times it ran and how long it took. $ psql -c \"SELECT calls, round(total_exec_time::numeric,0) AS total_ms, round(mean_exec_time::numeric,1) AS mean_ms, left(query,60) AS query FROM pg_stat_statements ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 3;\" calls | total_ms | mean_ms | query --------+----------+---------+-------------------------------------------------- 480211 | 9120432 | 19.0 | SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = $1 ORDER BY 1210 | 4411200 | 3645.5 | SELECT customer_id, sum(total) FROM orders WHERE cre Sort by total time, not by the slowest single run. A 19 ms query that runs 480 000 times costs more than a 3.6 s report that runs 1 200 times. The setting log_min_duration_statement = 500 writes any statement slower than 500 ms to the log, which catches queries your extension view has not reached yet. EXPLAIN EXPLAIN shows the plan the database chooses. EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) runs the query and shows what really happened. Do not use ANALYZE on a statement that changes data without a transaction you roll back. $ psql -c \"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;\" Seq Scan on orders (cost=0.00..412345.00 rows=11 width=96) (actual time=0.031..1893.2 rows=9 loops=1) Filter: (customer_id = 4711) Rows Removed by Filter: 9999991 Buffers: shared hit=1204 read=211141 Planning Time: 0.12 ms Execution Time: 1893.3 ms Read it from the inside out and look for: Seq Scan on a big table that returns a few rows. The database read ten million rows to find nine ( Rows Removed by Filter ). An index on customer_id would let it jump to them. Index Scan / Index Only Scan / Bitmap Heap Scan : the database uses an index. Fewer buffers read is the goal. Estimated rows vs actual rows : rows=11 versus rows=9 is fine. When the estimate is 10 and the actual is 2 000 000, the planner made its choice with wrong information. Run ANALYZE table; to refresh statistics. Sort with Sort Method: external merge Disk means the sort spilled to disk because work_mem was too small for that query. Nested Loop is good for few rows and terrible for many. Hash Join and Merge Join suit larger sets. Change one thing After a change, run the same EXPLAIN again and compare the time and the buffers. Keep the old plan text in your ticket, so a reviewer can see the difference. $ psql -c \"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_customer ON orders (customer_id);\" CREATE INDEX $ psql -c \"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711;\" | grep -E \"Index|Execution\" Index Scan using idx_orders_customer on orders (actual time=0.040..0.061 rows=9 loops=1) Execution Time: 0.09 ms CONCURRENTLY builds the index without blocking writes. It takes longer and cannot run inside a transaction block. Incident: The query that was fast yesterday Incident A checkout query went from 5 ms to 4 seconds overnight with no code change. $ psql -c \"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE) SELECT * FROM carts WHERE status=&#x27;open&#x27; AND user_id=88;\" Nested Loop (cost=0.85..120.40 rows=3 width=64) (actual time=0.03..4012.7 rows=118002 loops=1) $ psql -c \"SELECT relname, last_autoanalyze, n_live_tup FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname=&#x27;carts&#x27;;\" carts | 2026-10-19 02:11:04 | 3120 The planner expected 3 rows and found 118 002. The table had been loaded with a large import the night before, and statistics were two weeks old, so the planner chose a nested loop that suited a small table. ANALYZE carts; fixed the plan in seconds. Th"},"sql-ops/indexes-that-help-and-indexes-that-hurt":{"html":"\u003cp>An index is a trade. It makes some reads fast and makes every write slower and larger. Good index design means adding the few that matter and removing the ones that do not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How a B-tree index is used, and why column order matters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Composite, partial and covering indexes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The write cost of indexes and how to find unused ones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why an index is sometimes ignored\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-a-b-tree-works-for-you\">How a B-tree works for you\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A B-tree index keeps values sorted. It answers equality, ranges and \u003ccode>ORDER BY\u003c/code> on the indexed columns. With a \u003cstrong>composite\u003c/strong> index on \u003ccode>(customer_id, created_at)\u003c/code>, rows are sorted by customer first, then by date inside each customer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The rule of thumb for column order: columns tested for \u003cstrong>equality\u003c/strong> first, then the column used for a \u003cstrong>range or sort\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_created ON orders (customer_id, created_at);&quot;\npsql -c &quot;EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711 AND created_at &gt;= '2026-10-01' ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;&quot; | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_created ON orders (customer_id, created_at);&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711 AND created_at &gt;= &#x27;2026-10-01&#x27; ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Limit  (cost=0.57..38.10 rows=20 width=96)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   -&gt;  Index Scan Backward using idx_orders_cust_created on orders\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">         Index Cond: ((customer_id = 4711) AND (created_at &gt;= '2026-10-01'))\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This one index serves queries by customer alone, and by customer plus date, and also gives the sort for free. It does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> help a query that filters only on \u003ccode>created_at\u003c/code>, because the sorted order begins with customer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"partial-and-covering-indexes\">Partial and covering indexes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>partial index\u003c/strong> covers only some rows. If 2 percent of orders are \u003ccode>status=&#39;open&#39;\u003c/code> and queries look only for those, a smaller index is faster to scan and cheaper to maintain.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_open ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = 'open';\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_open ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = &#x27;open&#x27;;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>covering index\u003c/strong> adds extra columns so the database can answer from the index alone (an index-only scan):\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_total ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (total);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_total ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (total);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Index-only scans also need the table&#39;s visibility map to be current, which vacuum keeps up to date.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cost-of-indexes\">The cost of indexes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every insert updates every index on the table. Every update that changes an indexed column does too. Indexes also take disk and memory and slow vacuum. A table with twelve indexes may have write speed far below one with three.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Find indexes that are never used:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT relname, indexrelname, idx_scan, pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(indexrelid)) AS size FROM pg_stat_user_indexes ORDER BY idx_scan, pg_relation_size(indexrelid) DESC LIMIT 3;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT relname, indexrelname, idx_scan, pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(indexrelid)) AS size FROM pg_stat_user_indexes ORDER BY idx_scan, pg_relation_size(indexrelid) DESC LIMIT 3;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> relname | indexrelname        | idx_scan | size\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---------+---------------------+----------+--------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> orders  | idx_orders_status   |        0 | 1.8 GB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> orders  | idx_orders_created  |       12 | 1.6 GB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>idx_scan = 0\u003c/code> since the last statistics reset is a candidate for removal, but check first that the statistics cover a full business cycle (month end, reporting jobs) and that the index does not enforce a unique constraint. Drop with \u003ccode>DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Duplicate indexes are common too: an index on \u003ccode>(customer_id)\u003c/code> is redundant when \u003ccode>(customer_id, created_at)\u003c/code> exists, because the composite serves the same lookups.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-the-planner-ignores-an-index\">When the planner ignores an index\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The query returns a large part of the table, so reading the table directly is cheaper.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A function wraps the column: \u003ccode>WHERE lower(email) = &#39;a@b.c&#39;\u003c/code> cannot use an index on \u003ccode>email\u003c/code>. Create an expression index on \u003ccode>lower(email)\u003c/code>, or change the query.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The types differ and force a cast of the column.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Statistics are stale and the planner thinks the table is small.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A leading wildcard: \u003ccode>LIKE &#39;%smith&#39;\u003c/code> cannot use a plain B-tree.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-twenty-indexes-and-a-slow-import\">Incident: Twenty indexes and a slow import\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly import that took 20 minutes grew to 5 hours over a year.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_indexes WHERE tablename='events';&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_table_size('events')) AS table, pg_size_pretty(pg_indexes_size('events')) AS indexes;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_indexes WHERE tablename=&#x27;events&#x27;;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    23\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_table_size(&#x27;events&#x27;)) AS table, pg_size_pretty(pg_indexes_size(&#x27;events&#x27;)) AS indexes;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> table  | indexes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--------+---------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 41 GB  | 87 GB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Over time people added an index for each new report. The import had to update 23 indexes for every row, and the indexes were twice the size of the table. A review found nine unused and four duplicate indexes. Removing thirteen brought the import back under an hour. Each index had been a reasonable choice when it was added; nobody owned the total.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> The query is slow, so index every column in the WHERE clause.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>CREATE INDEX ON orders (status);\nCREATE INDEX ON orders (created_at);\nCREATE INDEX ON orders (customer_id);\nCREATE INDEX ON orders (total);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>(queries are a bit faster)\n$ \\di+ orders*\n4 indexes, 18 GB\ninserts: 1200/s down to 400/s\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Each index must be updated on every write. A low-selectivity index on status is never used, and the planner often ignores single-column indexes when a composite one would fit the query.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4821 AND status = 'open' ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;\nCREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY ON orders (customer_id, created_at DESC) WHERE status = 'open';\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Read the plan, design one index for the real query, and drop indexes that are never used.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Adding an index for every slow query without checking existing ones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting the range column before the equality column in a composite index\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Wrapping an indexed column in a function\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dropping an index that backs a unique or foreign key constraint\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-a-b-tree-works-for-you","text":"How a B-tree works for you"},{"id":"partial-and-covering-indexes","text":"Partial and covering indexes"},{"id":"the-cost-of-indexes","text":"The cost of indexes"},{"id":"when-the-planner-ignores-an-index","text":"When the planner ignores an index"},{"id":"incident-twenty-indexes-and-a-slow-import","text":"Incident: Twenty indexes and a slow import"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Indexes","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Multicolumn indexes","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-multicolumn.html"},{"t":"Use The Index, Luke","u":"https://use-the-index-luke.com/"}],"text":"An index is a trade. It makes some reads fast and makes every write slower and larger. Good index design means adding the few that matter and removing the ones that do not. What you will learn How a B-tree index is used, and why column order matters Composite, partial and covering indexes The write cost of indexes and how to find unused ones Why an index is sometimes ignored How a B-tree works for you A B-tree index keeps values sorted. It answers equality, ranges and ORDER BY on the indexed columns. With a composite index on (customer_id, created_at) , rows are sorted by customer first, then by date inside each customer. The rule of thumb for column order: columns tested for equality first, then the column used for a range or sort . $ psql -c \"CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_created ON orders (customer_id, created_at);\" $ psql -c \"EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE customer_id = 4711 AND created_at >= &#x27;2026-10-01&#x27; ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 20;\" | head -3 Limit (cost=0.57..38.10 rows=20 width=96) -> Index Scan Backward using idx_orders_cust_created on orders Index Cond: ((customer_id = 4711) AND (created_at >= '2026-10-01')) This one index serves queries by customer alone, and by customer plus date, and also gives the sort for free. It does not help a query that filters only on created_at , because the sorted order begins with customer. Partial and covering indexes A partial index covers only some rows. If 2 percent of orders are status=&#39;open&#39; and queries look only for those, a smaller index is faster to scan and cheaper to maintain. CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_open ON orders (customer_id) WHERE status = &#x27;open&#x27;; A covering index adds extra columns so the database can answer from the index alone (an index-only scan): CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_orders_cust_total ON orders (customer_id) INCLUDE (total); Index-only scans also need the table&#39;s visibility map to be current, which vacuum keeps up to date. The cost of indexes Every insert updates every index on the table. Every update that changes an indexed column does too. Indexes also take disk and memory and slow vacuum. A table with twelve indexes may have write speed far below one with three. Find indexes that are never used: $ psql -c \"SELECT relname, indexrelname, idx_scan, pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(indexrelid)) AS size FROM pg_stat_user_indexes ORDER BY idx_scan, pg_relation_size(indexrelid) DESC LIMIT 3;\" relname | indexrelname | idx_scan | size ---------+---------------------+----------+-------- orders | idx_orders_status | 0 | 1.8 GB orders | idx_orders_created | 12 | 1.6 GB idx_scan = 0 since the last statistics reset is a candidate for removal, but check first that the statistics cover a full business cycle (month end, reporting jobs) and that the index does not enforce a unique constraint. Drop with DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY . Duplicate indexes are common too: an index on (customer_id) is redundant when (customer_id, created_at) exists, because the composite serves the same lookups. When the planner ignores an index The query returns a large part of the table, so reading the table directly is cheaper. A function wraps the column: WHERE lower(email) = &#39;a@b.c&#39; cannot use an index on email . Create an expression index on lower(email) , or change the query. The types differ and force a cast of the column. Statistics are stale and the planner thinks the table is small. A leading wildcard: LIKE &#39;%smith&#39; cannot use a plain B-tree. Incident: Twenty indexes and a slow import Incident A nightly import that took 20 minutes grew to 5 hours over a year. $ psql -c \"SELECT count(*) FROM pg_indexes WHERE tablename=&#x27;events&#x27;;\" count ------- 23 $ psql -c \"SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_table_size(&#x27;events&#x27;)) AS table, pg_size_pretty(pg_indexes_size(&#x27;events&#x27;)) AS indexes;\" table | indexes --------+--------- 41 GB | 87 GB Over time people added an index for each new report. The import had t"},"sql-ops/locks-deadlocks-and-long-transactions":{"html":"\u003cp>A query can be slow because it is working, or because it is waiting. Locks are the usual reason for waiting, and one forgotten transaction can stop a whole application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Row locks, table locks and how they conflict\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding the blocker with pg_stat_activity and pg_blocking_pids\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Idle in transaction and why it is dangerous\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deadlocks and the timeouts that limit damage\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"locks-in-short\">Locks in short\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a transaction updates a row, it holds a lock on it until the transaction ends. Other writers to that row wait. Readers are not blocked, because PostgreSQL keeps old row versions for them (MVCC). Statements such as \u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code> take stronger table-level locks, and those can queue behind a long reader and then block everyone behind them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The key sentence: \u003cstrong>locks are held until COMMIT or ROLLBACK\u003c/strong>, not until the statement ends.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-blocker\">Find the blocker\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, wait_event, now()-xact_start AS tx_age, left(query,50) AS query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE datname='shop' ORDER BY xact_start;&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) &gt; 0;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, wait_event, now()-xact_start AS tx_age, left(query,50) AS query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE datname=&#x27;shop&#x27; ORDER BY xact_start;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pid  | state               | wait_event_type | wait_event    | tx_age   | query\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------+---------------------+-----------------+---------------+----------+------------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2210 | idle in transaction |                 |               | 03:12:44 | UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2488 | active              | Lock            | transactionid | 00:41:02 | UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10 WHERE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) &gt; 0;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pid  | blocked_by\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------+------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2488 | {2210}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Process 2488 waits for a lock held by 2210. Process 2210 is \u003cstrong>idle in transaction\u003c/strong>: it started a transaction, did an update, and then did nothing for three hours. The client opened a transaction and never finished it, often because of a bug, a crashed worker, or someone&#39;s session left open in a terminal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>You can stop the blocker with \u003ccode>SELECT pg_terminate_backend(2210);\u003c/code>. That rolls back its work, so ask who owns it if the data matters. \u003ccode>pg_cancel_backend(pid)\u003c/code> cancels only the running statement and is gentler.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"long-transactions-hurt-in-other-ways\">Long transactions hurt in other ways\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A transaction that stays open holds back the cleanup of old row versions across the database, so tables grow and vacuum cannot remove dead rows. It also holds locks and may keep a replica in conflict. Keep transactions short, and never keep one open while waiting for a person or a slow outside call.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deadlocks\">Deadlocks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>deadlock\u003c/strong> happens when two transactions each hold a lock the other needs. PostgreSQL detects it after \u003ccode>deadlock_timeout\u003c/code> (1 second by default), aborts one of them with an error, and lets the other continue.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR:  deadlock detected\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">DETAIL:  Process 2210 waits for ShareLock on transaction 8841; blocked by process 2488.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">         Process 2488 waits for ShareLock on transaction 8839; blocked by process 2210.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HINT:  See server log for query details.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The cure is in the application: touch rows in the same order everywhere (for example, always update the account with the lower id first), keep transactions short, and retry the failed transaction.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"timeouts-that-protect-you\">Timeouts that protect you\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>lock_timeout\u003c/code>: how long a statement waits for a lock before giving up. Set it for migrations, so an \u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code> that cannot get its lock fails quickly instead of queueing behind a reader and freezing the table.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>idle_in_transaction_session_timeout\u003c/code>: ends sessions that sit in a transaction doing nothing.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>statement_timeout\u003c/code>: caps how long a single statement may run.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = '60s';\nALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = '30s';\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = &#x27;60s&#x27;;\nALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = &#x27;30s&#x27;;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-migration-that-froze-checkout\">Incident: The migration that froze checkout\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A one-line schema change brought checkout to a halt for four minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, now()-query_start AS age, left(query,45) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state &lt;&gt; 'idle' ORDER BY query_start LIMIT 3;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, now()-query_start AS age, left(query,45) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state &lt;&gt; &#x27;idle&#x27; ORDER BY query_start LIMIT 3;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> pid  | state  | wait_event_type | age      | left\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------+--------+-----------------+----------+---------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1190 | active |                 | 00:04:10 | SELECT count(*) FROM orders  -- report\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1304 | active | Lock            | 00:03:55 | ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN note text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 1350 | active | Lock            | 00:03:50 | INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, total)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A long report held a weak lock on \u003ccode>orders\u003c/code>. The \u003ccode>ALTER TABLE\u003c/code> needed a strong lock and queued behind it. Every new insert then queued behind the \u003ccode>ALTER\u003c/code>, so checkout stopped even though the report only read data. Setting \u003ccode>lock_timeout = &#39;3s&#39;\u003c/code> for the migration lets it fail fast and be retried in a quiet moment, and the report was moved to a replica.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running migrations with no lock_timeout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving transactions open while waiting for user input or HTTP calls\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Killing the victim instead of the blocker\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring deadlock errors instead of fixing lock order and retrying\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"locks-in-short","text":"Locks in short"},{"id":"find-the-blocker","text":"Find the blocker"},{"id":"long-transactions-hurt-in-other-ways","text":"Long transactions hurt in other ways"},{"id":"deadlocks","text":"Deadlocks"},{"id":"timeouts-that-protect-you","text":"Timeouts that protect you"},{"id":"incident-the-migration-that-froze-checkout","text":"Incident: The migration that froze checkout"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Explicit locking","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Monitoring activity","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Server configuration, client connection defaults","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-client.html"}],"text":"A query can be slow because it is working, or because it is waiting. Locks are the usual reason for waiting, and one forgotten transaction can stop a whole application. What you will learn Row locks, table locks and how they conflict Finding the blocker with pg_stat_activity and pg_blocking_pids Idle in transaction and why it is dangerous Deadlocks and the timeouts that limit damage Locks in short When a transaction updates a row, it holds a lock on it until the transaction ends. Other writers to that row wait. Readers are not blocked, because PostgreSQL keeps old row versions for them (MVCC). Statements such as ALTER TABLE take stronger table-level locks, and those can queue behind a long reader and then block everyone behind them. The key sentence: locks are held until COMMIT or ROLLBACK , not until the statement ends. Find the blocker $ psql -c \"SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, wait_event, now()-xact_start AS tx_age, left(query,50) AS query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE datname=&#x27;shop&#x27; ORDER BY xact_start;\" pid | state | wait_event_type | wait_event | tx_age | query ------+---------------------+-----------------+---------------+----------+------------------------------------------------ 2210 | idle in transaction | | | 03:12:44 | UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE 2488 | active | Lock | transactionid | 00:41:02 | UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10 WHERE $ psql -c \"SELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) AS blocked_by FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) > 0;\" pid | blocked_by ------+------------ 2488 | {2210} Process 2488 waits for a lock held by 2210. Process 2210 is idle in transaction : it started a transaction, did an update, and then did nothing for three hours. The client opened a transaction and never finished it, often because of a bug, a crashed worker, or someone&#39;s session left open in a terminal. You can stop the blocker with SELECT pg_terminate_backend(2210); . That rolls back its work, so ask who owns it if the data matters. pg_cancel_backend(pid) cancels only the running statement and is gentler. Long transactions hurt in other ways A transaction that stays open holds back the cleanup of old row versions across the database, so tables grow and vacuum cannot remove dead rows. It also holds locks and may keep a replica in conflict. Keep transactions short, and never keep one open while waiting for a person or a slow outside call. Deadlocks A deadlock happens when two transactions each hold a lock the other needs. PostgreSQL detects it after deadlock_timeout (1 second by default), aborts one of them with an error, and lets the other continue. ERROR: deadlock detected DETAIL: Process 2210 waits for ShareLock on transaction 8841; blocked by process 2488. Process 2488 waits for ShareLock on transaction 8839; blocked by process 2210. HINT: See server log for query details. The cure is in the application: touch rows in the same order everywhere (for example, always update the account with the lower id first), keep transactions short, and retry the failed transaction. Timeouts that protect you lock_timeout : how long a statement waits for a lock before giving up. Set it for migrations, so an ALTER TABLE that cannot get its lock fails quickly instead of queueing behind a reader and freezing the table. idle_in_transaction_session_timeout : ends sessions that sit in a transaction doing nothing. statement_timeout : caps how long a single statement may run. ALTER ROLE app SET idle_in_transaction_session_timeout = &#x27;60s&#x27;; ALTER ROLE app SET statement_timeout = &#x27;30s&#x27;; Incident: The migration that froze checkout Incident A one-line schema change brought checkout to a halt for four minutes. $ psql -c \"SELECT pid, state, wait_event_type, now()-query_start AS age, left(query,45) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state \u003c> &#x27;idle&#x27; ORDER BY query_start LIMIT 3;\" pid | state | wait_event_type | age | left ------+--------+-----------------+----------+-------"},"sql-ops/connections-pooling-and-capacity":{"html":"\u003cp>A database does not slow down smoothly when it is overloaded. It runs fine, then it runs out of connections or memory, and fails quickly. The usual cause is that every application instance opens its own pool of connections.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why PostgreSQL connections are expensive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Connection pool maths for an application fleet\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>PgBouncer and its pooling modes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Memory settings: shared_buffers, work_mem and their traps\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-connections-cost\">Why connections cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>PostgreSQL starts a separate process for each client connection. Each one uses memory and a slot in shared structures, and having hundreds of active sessions makes the CPUs spend time switching between them. A server with 16 cores often performs best with a few dozen running queries, not several hundred.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SHOW max_connections;&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SHOW max_connections;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> max_connections\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-----------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> state                | count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">----------------------+-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> active               |    14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> idle                 |   171\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> idle in transaction  |     9\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Most of the 194 sessions are idle. They are still using memory and taking slots. When the limit is reached, new clients get:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FATAL:  sorry, too many clients already\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pool-maths\">Pool maths\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each application process has its own pool. With 40 pods and a pool of 10 connections each, the application can open 400 connections, which is more than the database allows. Add a second deployment, a cron job and a reporting tool and the limit is crossed with no one noticing, until a deploy restarts all pods at once and every pod reconnects together.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Work out the total: \u003cstrong>instances × pool size\u003c/strong> across every client, and keep it below \u003ccode>max_connections\u003c/code> minus the reserved slots for administrators (\u003ccode>superuser_reserved_connections\u003c/code>, 3 by default).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pgbouncer\">PgBouncer\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>pooler\u003c/strong> sits between applications and the database. Applications connect to it by the thousands, and it keeps a small number of real connections.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Session pooling\u003c/strong>: a server connection is assigned for the life of the client connection. Safe, but saves little if clients stay connected.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Transaction pooling\u003c/strong>: a server connection is assigned only during a transaction. This is the most useful mode, and it limits what the application may use: session-level features such as \u003ccode>SET\u003c/code>, advisory locks, \u003ccode>LISTEN\u003c/code> and (in older versions) prepared statements may not behave as expected.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Statement pooling\u003c/strong>: one statement at a time. Rarely usable, as it forbids multi-statement transactions.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[databases]\nshop = host=10.0.2.10 port=5432 dbname=shop\n\n[pgbouncer]\npool_mode = transaction\nmax_client_conn = 2000\ndefault_pool_size = 20\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">[databases]\nshop = host=10.0.2.10 port=5432 dbname=shop\n\n[pgbouncer]\npool_mode = transaction\nmax_client_conn = 2000\ndefault_pool_size = 20\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With these settings 2 000 clients can connect and at most 20 server connections per database and user pair are used.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-settings\">Memory settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>shared_buffers\u003c/strong>: the shared cache. A common starting point is about a quarter of RAM on a dedicated server.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>work_mem\u003c/strong>: memory for \u003cstrong>each\u003c/strong> sort or hash step in \u003cstrong>each\u003c/strong> query. It is not per connection but per operation, so a complex query can use several times this value, and 200 connections doing so can add up to many gigabytes. Raising it globally is risky; raise it per role or per session for the reporting job.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>maintenance_work_mem\u003c/strong>: used by vacuum and index builds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>effective_cache_size\u003c/strong>: tells the planner how much the OS is likely to cache. It allocates nothing.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-everyone-reconnected-at-once\">Incident: Everyone reconnected at once\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a deploy the database stopped accepting connections for ten minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;&quot;\npsql -c &quot;SELECT application_name, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 2;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FATAL:  sorry, too many clients already\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT application_name, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 2;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> shop-api     | 170\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> shop-worker  |  26\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The API had 17 pods with a pool of 10 connections each, and a rolling deploy started new pods before closing the old ones, so for a while there were twice as many. The pool fills with failed connection attempts, requests retry, and the load made recovery slower. The team added PgBouncer in transaction mode, set the pool per pod to 5, and limited the surge during deploys.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> The database is out of connections, so raise max_connections to 5000.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>ALTER SYSTEM SET max_connections = 5000;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>(after restart)\n$ free -m\nMemory used 98%\nlatency up, OOM killer ran\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Each connection is a process with its own memory, and many active connections cause contention. Raising the limit hides the problem until the server runs out of memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># keep max_connections modest and add a pooler\npool_mode = transaction\ndefault_pool_size = 20\nmax_client_conn = 2000\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Use a pooler so a few server connections serve many clients, and find the leak or idle in transaction sessions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Raising max_connections instead of fixing the pool sizes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting work_mem very high globally\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using transaction pooling with an application that depends on session state\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not leaving reserved connections for administrators\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"why-connections-cost","text":"Why connections cost"},{"id":"pool-maths","text":"Pool maths"},{"id":"pgbouncer","text":"PgBouncer"},{"id":"memory-settings","text":"Memory settings"},{"id":"incident-everyone-reconnected-at-once","text":"Incident: Everyone reconnected at once"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: Connections and authentication","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-connection.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Resource consumption","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-resource.html"},{"t":"PgBouncer documentation","u":"https://www.pgbouncer.org/config.html"}],"text":"A database does not slow down smoothly when it is overloaded. It runs fine, then it runs out of connections or memory, and fails quickly. The usual cause is that every application instance opens its own pool of connections. What you will learn Why PostgreSQL connections are expensive Connection pool maths for an application fleet PgBouncer and its pooling modes Memory settings: shared_buffers, work_mem and their traps Why connections cost PostgreSQL starts a separate process for each client connection. Each one uses memory and a slot in shared structures, and having hundreds of active sessions makes the CPUs spend time switching between them. A server with 16 cores often performs best with a few dozen running queries, not several hundred. $ psql -c \"SHOW max_connections;\" max_connections ----------------- 200 $ psql -c \"SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;\" state | count ----------------------+------- active | 14 idle | 171 idle in transaction | 9 Most of the 194 sessions are idle. They are still using memory and taking slots. When the limit is reached, new clients get: FATAL: sorry, too many clients already Pool maths Each application process has its own pool. With 40 pods and a pool of 10 connections each, the application can open 400 connections, which is more than the database allows. Add a second deployment, a cron job and a reporting tool and the limit is crossed with no one noticing, until a deploy restarts all pods at once and every pod reconnects together. Work out the total: instances × pool size across every client, and keep it below max_connections minus the reserved slots for administrators ( superuser_reserved_connections , 3 by default). PgBouncer A pooler sits between applications and the database. Applications connect to it by the thousands, and it keeps a small number of real connections. Session pooling : a server connection is assigned for the life of the client connection. Safe, but saves little if clients stay connected. Transaction pooling : a server connection is assigned only during a transaction. This is the most useful mode, and it limits what the application may use: session-level features such as SET , advisory locks, LISTEN and (in older versions) prepared statements may not behave as expected. Statement pooling : one statement at a time. Rarely usable, as it forbids multi-statement transactions. [databases] shop = host=10.0.2.10 port=5432 dbname=shop [pgbouncer] pool_mode = transaction max_client_conn = 2000 default_pool_size = 20 With these settings 2 000 clients can connect and at most 20 server connections per database and user pair are used. Memory settings shared_buffers : the shared cache. A common starting point is about a quarter of RAM on a dedicated server. work_mem : memory for each sort or hash step in each query. It is not per connection but per operation, so a complex query can use several times this value, and 200 connections doing so can add up to many gigabytes. Raising it globally is risky; raise it per role or per session for the reporting job. maintenance_work_mem : used by vacuum and index builds. effective_cache_size : tells the planner how much the OS is likely to cache. It allocates nothing. Incident: Everyone reconnected at once Incident After a deploy the database stopped accepting connections for ten minutes. FATAL: sorry, too many clients already $ psql -c \"SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity;\" count ------- 200 $ psql -c \"SELECT application_name, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 2;\" shop-api | 170 shop-worker | 26 The API had 17 pods with a pool of 10 connections each, and a rolling deploy started new pods before closing the old ones, so for a while there were twice as many. The pool fills with failed connection attempts, requests retry, and the load made recovery slower. The team added PgBouncer in transaction mode, set the pool per pod to 5, and limited the surge during deploys. Worked example: a tempting wron"},"sql-ops/replication-lag-vacuum-and-day-two-operations":{"html":"\u003cp>A database that works on launch day needs care for years: copies must keep up, old rows must be cleaned out, backups must restore, and changes must not hurt users.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Streaming replication and how to measure lag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Vacuum, autovacuum and table bloat\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe schema changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups, point-in-time recovery and restore tests\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"replication-and-lag\">Replication and lag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>primary\u003c/strong> sends its write-ahead log (WAL) to \u003cstrong>standbys\u003c/strong>, which replay it. Lag is the delay before a change on the primary is visible on a standby. Reading from a standby that is behind can show old data, such as a user&#39;s order missing right after it was placed.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">primary$ psql -c &quot;SELECT client_addr, state, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind, replay_lag FROM pg_stat_replication;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> client_addr | state     | bytes_behind | replay_lag\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------------+-----------+--------------+-----------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 10.0.2.21   | streaming |   1572864000 | 00:07:41.28\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">standby$ psql -c &quot;SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS delay;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> delay\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-----------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 00:07:44.1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Common causes of lag: a heavy write burst, a slow standby disk, a long query on the standby that conflicts with replay (the setting \u003ccode>max_standby_streaming_delay\u003c/code> decides who gives way), or the network. A \u003cstrong>replication slot\u003c/strong> keeps WAL for a standby that is offline, and if the standby never returns the primary&#39;s disk fills with WAL. Monitor slots and drop abandoned ones.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vacuum-and-bloat\">Vacuum and bloat\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a row is updated or deleted, the old version stays until \u003cstrong>vacuum\u003c/strong> removes it. \u003cstrong>Autovacuum\u003c/strong> runs vacuum automatically when about 20 percent of a table (the \u003ccode>autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor\u003c/code>) plus a small base has changed. On a table with 500 million rows that means 100 million dead rows before it starts, which is far too late. Lower the factor for big, busy tables:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ALTER TABLE events SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02);\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ALTER TABLE events SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02);\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -c &quot;SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 2;&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 2;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> relname | n_live_tup | n_dead_tup | last_autovacuum\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">---------+------------+------------+---------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> events  |  412000000 |   96000000 | 2026-09-30 03:12:10\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Vacuum cannot remove a row version that some open transaction might still see, so a long transaction (see the locks lesson) keeps dead rows alive. \u003cstrong>Bloat\u003c/strong> is the wasted space left behind. \u003ccode>VACUUM\u003c/code> marks it reusable; it does not shrink the file. To shrink a table, use \u003ccode>pg_repack\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>VACUUM FULL\u003c/code> (which locks the table).\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A related risk is \u003cstrong>transaction ID wraparound\u003c/strong>: transaction numbers are finite, and vacuum must freeze old rows. If autovacuum cannot keep up, PostgreSQL eventually refuses writes to protect data. Monitor \u003ccode>age(datfrozenxid)\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-schema-changes\">Safe schema changes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Add columns as nullable with no default or with a constant default (cheap on current versions), then backfill in batches.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Create indexes with \u003ccode>CONCURRENTLY\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add constraints as \u003ccode>NOT VALID\u003c/code> and validate later, so the lock is short.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set \u003ccode>lock_timeout\u003c/code> for the migration, and run it when traffic is low.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep changes backward compatible, so old and new application versions both work during a deploy.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backups\">Backups\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Logical\u003c/strong> backups (\u003ccode>pg_dump\u003c/code>) are portable and slow to restore on big databases.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Physical\u003c/strong> backups with WAL archiving (tools such as pgBackRest) allow \u003cstrong>point-in-time recovery\u003c/strong>: restore to the minute before the bad change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication is not a backup: a mistaken \u003ccode>DELETE\u003c/code> replicates to every standby in seconds.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Decide RPO and RTO, then restore to a scratch server regularly and run a query that checks the data.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-standby-that-was-always-behind\">Incident: The standby that was always behind\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Customers saw their new orders missing for several minutes, and then the primary ran out of disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">primary$ psql -c &quot;SELECT slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained FROM pg_replication_slots;&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> slot_name    | active | retained\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">--------------+--------+----------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> reporting_s1 | f      | 412 GB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A reporting replica had been switched off a week earlier but its replication slot remained. The primary kept every WAL file since then, 412 GB, until the disk filled. Another standby was lagging because a long report on it conflicted with replay. Dropping the dead slot with \u003ccode>pg_drop_replication_slot\u003c/code> freed the disk, and reports were moved to a replica with its own conflict settings. Replication needs monitoring just as the primary does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading important data from a lagging standby\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving autovacuum defaults on very large tables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Believing a replica is a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never restoring a backup to see if it works\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"replication-and-lag","text":"Replication and lag"},{"id":"vacuum-and-bloat","text":"Vacuum and bloat"},{"id":"safe-schema-changes","text":"Safe schema changes"},{"id":"backups","text":"Backups"},{"id":"incident-the-standby-that-was-always-behind","text":"Incident: The standby that was always behind"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"PostgreSQL: High availability and replication","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/high-availability.html"},{"t":"PostgreSQL: Routine vacuuming","u":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/routine-vacuuming.html"},{"t":"pgBackRest documentation","u":"https://pgbackrest.org/user-guide.html"}],"text":"A database that works on launch day needs care for years: copies must keep up, old rows must be cleaned out, backups must restore, and changes must not hurt users. What you will learn Streaming replication and how to measure lag Vacuum, autovacuum and table bloat Safe schema changes Backups, point-in-time recovery and restore tests Replication and lag A primary sends its write-ahead log (WAL) to standbys , which replay it. Lag is the delay before a change on the primary is visible on a standby. Reading from a standby that is behind can show old data, such as a user&#39;s order missing right after it was placed. primary$ psql -c \"SELECT client_addr, state, pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn) AS bytes_behind, replay_lag FROM pg_stat_replication;\" client_addr | state | bytes_behind | replay_lag -------------+-----------+--------------+----------------- 10.0.2.21 | streaming | 1572864000 | 00:07:41.28 standby$ psql -c \"SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS delay;\" delay ----------------- 00:07:44.1 Common causes of lag: a heavy write burst, a slow standby disk, a long query on the standby that conflicts with replay (the setting max_standby_streaming_delay decides who gives way), or the network. A replication slot keeps WAL for a standby that is offline, and if the standby never returns the primary&#39;s disk fills with WAL. Monitor slots and drop abandoned ones. Vacuum and bloat When a row is updated or deleted, the old version stays until vacuum removes it. Autovacuum runs vacuum automatically when about 20 percent of a table (the autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor ) plus a small base has changed. On a table with 500 million rows that means 100 million dead rows before it starts, which is far too late. Lower the factor for big, busy tables: ALTER TABLE events SET (autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor = 0.02); $ psql -c \"SELECT relname, n_live_tup, n_dead_tup, last_autovacuum FROM pg_stat_user_tables ORDER BY n_dead_tup DESC LIMIT 2;\" relname | n_live_tup | n_dead_tup | last_autovacuum ---------+------------+------------+--------------------- events | 412000000 | 96000000 | 2026-09-30 03:12:10 Vacuum cannot remove a row version that some open transaction might still see, so a long transaction (see the locks lesson) keeps dead rows alive. Bloat is the wasted space left behind. VACUUM marks it reusable; it does not shrink the file. To shrink a table, use pg_repack or VACUUM FULL (which locks the table). A related risk is transaction ID wraparound : transaction numbers are finite, and vacuum must freeze old rows. If autovacuum cannot keep up, PostgreSQL eventually refuses writes to protect data. Monitor age(datfrozenxid) . Safe schema changes Add columns as nullable with no default or with a constant default (cheap on current versions), then backfill in batches. Create indexes with CONCURRENTLY . Add constraints as NOT VALID and validate later, so the lock is short. Set lock_timeout for the migration, and run it when traffic is low. Keep changes backward compatible, so old and new application versions both work during a deploy. Backups Logical backups ( pg_dump ) are portable and slow to restore on big databases. Physical backups with WAL archiving (tools such as pgBackRest) allow point-in-time recovery : restore to the minute before the bad change. Replication is not a backup: a mistaken DELETE replicates to every standby in seconds. Decide RPO and RTO, then restore to a scratch server regularly and run a query that checks the data. Incident: The standby that was always behind Incident Customers saw their new orders missing for several minutes, and then the primary ran out of disk. primary$ psql -c \"SELECT slot_name, active, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), restart_lsn)) AS retained FROM pg_replication_slots;\" slot_name | active | retained --------------+--------+---------- reporting_s1 | f | 412 GB A reporting replica had been switched off a week earlier but its replication slot remained. The primary kept ev"}});